1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:06,040
Greetings and welcome to this lecture in which I will be discussing the general principles

2
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:12,360
pertaining to biosafety levels and risk groups as described in the World Health Organization

3
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:18,500
Laboratory Biosafety Manual.

4
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:26,260
The WHO Laboratory Biosafety Manual categorizes biological agents into four distinct risk

5
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:36,620
groups based on their ability to cause harm to healthy human adults and their capacity

6
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:42,700
to be transmitted into the community.

7
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:49,460
Biological agents belonging to risk group 1 pose a low risk to the individual and a low

8
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:53,300
risk of transmission to the community.

9
00:00:53,300 --> 00:01:00,620
They may cause mild symptoms however this does not lead to morbidity or mortality.

10
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:08,700
Biological agents categorized as risk group 2 pose a significant threat to the individual

11
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:15,060
however this is moderate and the treatment is generally available.

12
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:22,240
The risk of transmission to the community is low.

13
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:29,720
Biological agents categorized as risk group 3 may cause severe infections in an individual

14
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:38,080
leading to morbidity or morbidity however therapies are available and the risk of transmission

15
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:43,630
to the community is low.

16
00:01:43,630 --> 00:01:51,950
Pathogens categorized as risk group 4 pose a high threat to the individual as well as

17
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:53,910
to the community.

18
00:01:53,910 --> 00:02:00,950
In this case therapeutic measures such as vaccines and other therapies may not be available

19
00:02:00,950 --> 00:02:13,810
which may lead to mass, morbidity and mortality.

20
00:02:13,810 --> 00:02:22,570
The WHO Laboratory Biosafety Manual specifies biosafety levels and this biosafety level

21
00:02:22,570 --> 00:02:28,450
must be considered when designing and developing your own respective containment facilities

22
00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:37,160
as well as the laboratory biosafety manuals which accompany these facilities.

23
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:42,720
In order to understand the concept of the containment facility one needs to look at what

24
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:45,640
is termed as directional air flow.

25
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:53,240
A contained facility comprises of physical space and within this physical space the

26
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:59,600
air pressure is maintained at negative with reference to the external environment.

27
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:07,880
This ensures that air flows into the facility and when it exits this facility it flows

28
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:13,110
out through a HEPA filter.

29
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:22,910
The personnel in this facility are protected by the flow of air however they must apply

30
00:03:22,910 --> 00:03:28,830
suitable measures such as the dawning of personal protective equipment in order to

31
00:03:28,830 --> 00:03:34,630
ensure that the risk of exposure to the biological agent is minimized.

32
00:03:34,630 --> 00:03:42,110
In a general containment facility the air is directed into the facility via HEPA filters

33
00:03:42,110 --> 00:03:46,230
and enters into the facility as filtered air.

34
00:03:46,230 --> 00:03:52,190
It then flows through the facility over the biological agent and is filtered prior to

35
00:03:52,190 --> 00:03:54,910
release into the environment.

36
00:03:54,910 --> 00:04:00,550
This ensures that the criteria for secondary containment are met and that the community

37
00:04:00,550 --> 00:04:11,440
is not exposed to any escaped biological agent from this particular facility.

38
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:18,680
The general layout for a biosafety level 2 facility is depicted in this particular

39
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:20,960
diagram.

40
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:27,080
This image has been derived from the World Health Organization laboratory biosafety manual

41
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:30,240
3rd edition.

42
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:36,040
The facility contains appropriate instrumentation.

43
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:41,120
This is an autoclave which is located at the entrance to this facility.

44
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:48,200
The door has appropriate signage and may be equipped with a secure keypad facility or

45
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:58,520
a fingerprint identification facility in order to address the risk posed by biosecurity.

46
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:05,280
The next aspect of this lab is the location of the biological safety cabinet which is

47
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:08,120
over here and the fume hood.

48
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:11,720
Both of these are located at the end of the facility.

49
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:13,200
This is the far end.

50
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:19,360
Now this ensures that all the air entering this facility flows over the floor and the

51
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:26,200
personnel prior to exiting in from this facility via the HEPA filters.

52
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:31,600
Both of these cabinets have to be hard ducted into the building and incorporated into the

53
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:34,200
building design.

54
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:39,000
There is other equipment in this facility such as the receptacles for the disposal

55
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:46,360
of waste, chemical, biological, radiological as the signage indicates as well as appropriate

56
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:55,900
workbenches for conducting laboratory operations.

57
00:05:55,900 --> 00:06:01,300
So this is the autoclave, the biological safety cabinet and the fume hood and their

58
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:07,020
respective placement in the laboratory workspace.

59
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:14,900
A biosafety level 3 laboratory or BSL 3 has additional components which one must incorporate

60
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:16,420
into the design.

61
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:20,180
The first aspect is the door.

62
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:26,380
So this door is equipped with a requisite signage as in a BSL 2.

63
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:32,820
However, there is additional security precaution in the form of a keypad or an identification

64
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:33,820
device.

65
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:40,060
Adjustment to this primary entry point of this door is the vestibule.

66
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:47,340
This vestibule serves as an airlock which permits equalization of pressure when an operator

67
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:49,860
enters into this facility.

68
00:06:49,860 --> 00:06:56,180
One can note that there is directional airflow as described in the slide on directional airflow.

69
00:06:56,180 --> 00:07:04,020
And this facility is maintained at negative pressure with reference to the external environment.

70
00:07:04,020 --> 00:07:10,700
Note that the location of the biological safety cabinets as well as the fume hood

71
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:17,060
is at the far end of the facility and the autoclave has been shifted into the facility

72
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:18,300
itself.

73
00:07:18,300 --> 00:07:25,260
All of this equipment is hard ducted into the system and the exhaust vents out into

74
00:07:25,260 --> 00:07:29,460
the external environment via HEPA filters.

75
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:39,340
The personnel working in a BSL 3 must don the requisite personal protective equipment

76
00:07:39,340 --> 00:07:47,380
which accords respiratory protection as well as other protection as the case may be.

77
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:53,020
This is determined by the risk assessment of that particular biological agent.

78
00:07:53,020 --> 00:07:57,580
The rest of the equipment is similar to a BSL 2.

79
00:07:57,580 --> 00:08:02,980
You can see the location of the receptacles as well as the storage cabinets and other

80
00:08:02,980 --> 00:08:06,100
accessory equipment in the facility.

81
00:08:06,100 --> 00:08:17,280
That is the BSC, the fume hood and the autoclave and the double door with the secure entry

82
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:21,240
and exit and the directional airflow.

83
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:28,960
These are the components which are required in order for a laboratory to be categorized

84
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:33,700
as a BSL 3 facility.

85
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:39,180
One of the questions which has been posed by many of the bioresk managers and biosafety

86
00:08:39,180 --> 00:08:48,900
officers is do I need to work in a BSL 3 facility when my pathogen has been categorized

87
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:50,820
as risk group 3?

88
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:56,420
And the answer is it depends on your administrative controls.

89
00:08:56,420 --> 00:09:05,620
You can work with a risk group 3 pathogen in a biosafety level 2 laboratory, however,

90
00:09:05,620 --> 00:09:13,180
you must implement the appropriate administrative controls in terms of standard operating procedures.

91
00:09:13,180 --> 00:09:20,900
Please note that this must be done on a case by case basis after a thorough risk assessment.

92
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:28,060
I hope this lecture has clarified some of the points regarding the risk groups and

93
00:09:28,060 --> 00:09:29,060
the facilities.

94
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:33,220
Thank you very much for watching and stay biosafe.
